{"id":"f15t1","name":"Access Logs Project","lang":"pyspark","entry":"solve","params":["spark","input_dir"],"limit_s":30,"ordered":true,"desc":"<p>This task is a small project. The editor contains a package <code>pipeline</code> with two files: <code>parse.py</code>, where you parse one log line, and <code>report.py</code>, where <code>solve</code> builds the report. Only <code>solve</code> is tested, but keep the parsing in <code>parse.py</code> and import it. Use the file list on the left of the editor to switch between files.</p>\n<p>Our web servers write one line per HTTP request, in files ending with <code>.log</code> placed at any depth of <code>input_dir</code>. A line looks like this:</p>\n<pre>203.0.113.5 - - [04/Mar/2026:10:12:01 +0100] \"GET /api/orders/42?full=1 HTTP/1.1\" 200 512 \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"</pre>\n<p>that is the client address, two dashes (or a user name instead of the second dash), the time with the offset of the server's time zone between square brackets, the request (method, path, protocol) between quotes, the status code, the size of the response in bytes (<code>-</code> when nothing was sent, which means 0), the referer and the user agent between quotes. The servers are in different time zones. Lines that do not follow this format (truncated lines, start-up messages) must be ignored, and so must requests from robots, whose user agent contains <code>bot</code> in any case.</p>\n<p>The SRE team wants traffic per <b>endpoint</b>, not per URL: the endpoint of a request is its path without the query string (everything from <code>?</code>), without a trailing <code>/</code> (except for the path <code>/</code> itself), and with every path segment made only of digits replaced by <code>{id}</code>. For example <code>/api/orders/42/?full=1</code> and <code>/api/orders/7</code> are both <code>/api/orders/{id}</code>.</p>\n<p>Your task is to return, for each hour in <b>UTC</b> and endpoint, the columns <code>hour</code> (string <code>yyyy-MM-dd HH:00</code>), <code>endpoint</code>, <code>requests</code> (long), <code>errors</code> (long, requests with a status of 500 or more) and <code>bytes</code> (long, total size), sorted by <code>hour</code>, then <code>endpoint</code>.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4><p class=\"exf\"><code>input_dir/eu/access.log</code></p><pre class=\"exv\">10.0.0.1 - - [04/Mar/2026:10:12:01 +0100] \"GET /api/orders/42/?full=1 HTTP/1.1\" 200 512 \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"\n10.0.0.2 - - [04/Mar/2026:09:59:00 +0000] \"GET /api/orders/7 HTTP/1.1\" 503 - \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"\n10.0.0.3 - ana [04/Mar/2026:10:05:00 +0100] \"GET / HTTP/1.1\" 200 100 \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"\n10.0.0.4 - - [04/Mar/2026:10:06:00 +0100] \"GET /api/orders/42 HTTP/1.1\" 200 300 \"-\" \"Googlebot/2.1\"\nserver started on :8080\n10.0.0.9 - - [04/Mar/2026:10:0\n</pre><p class=\"exf\"><code>input_dir/us/2026/03/access.log</code></p><pre class=\"exv\">10.1.0.1 - - [04/Mar/2026:04:30:00 -0500] \"POST /api/users/12/orders/3 HTTP/1.1\" 500 80 \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"\n10.1.0.2 - - [04/Mar/2026:09:10:00 -0500] \"GET /api/users/v2 HTTP/1.1\" 200 40 \"-\" \"curl/8.0\"\n</pre><p class=\"exf\"><code>input_dir/us/notes.txt</code></p><pre class=\"exv\">rotate weekly\n</pre><p>the output should be</p><div class=\"tblw\"><table><tr><th>hour</th><th>endpoint</th><th>requests</th><th>errors</th><th>bytes</th></tr><tr><td>2026-03-04 09:00</td><td>/</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>2026-03-04 09:00</td><td>/api/orders/{id}</td><td>2</td><td>1</td><td>512</td></tr><tr><td>2026-03-04 09:00</td><td>/api/users/{id}/orders/{id}</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>80</td></tr><tr><td>2026-03-04 14:00</td><td>/api/users/v2</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>40</td></tr></table></div><div class=\"lim\"><b>[execution time limit]</b> 30 seconds (pyspark)<br><b>[memory limit]</b> 2 GB<br><b>[input]</b> string input_dir (the path of a folder)<br><b>[output]</b> DataFrame with exactly the columns <code>hour</code>, <code>endpoint</code>, <code>requests</code>, <code>errors</code>, <code>bytes</code>, in this order; rows in the order given by the statement</div>","starter":"# ==== pipeline/parse.py ====\ndef parse_line(line):\n    \"\"\"Parse ONE log line.\n    Return None for a line to ignore, else a dict with the keys\n    hour (yyyy-MM-dd HH:00, UTC), endpoint, status (int) and bytes (int).\"\"\"\n    # write your code here\n    return None\n\n# ==== pipeline/report.py ====\nfrom pyspark.sql import SparkSession, DataFrame, functions as F\nfrom pipeline.parse import parse_line\n\n\ndef solve(spark: SparkSession, input_dir: str) -> DataFrame:\n    # write your code here\n    return spark.read.text(input_dir)\n","hints":[],"summary_fr":"","tags":["projet multi-fichiers","consigne longue","regex","fuseaux horaires","normalisation des chemins"],"minutes":25,"source":"Kata Lab","project":{"files":["pipeline/parse.py","pipeline/report.py"],"main":"pipeline/report.py"},"howto":"<h5>Où écrire</h5><ol><li>Le code est réparti en plusieurs fichiers, listés à gauche de l'éditeur sous <b>Code</b>. Clique sur un nom pour l'ouvrir.</li><li>Les tests appellent <code>solve(spark, input_dir)</code>, dans <code>pipeline/report.py</code>. Écris les fonctions d'aide dans <code>pipeline/parse.py</code> et importe-les comme dans le code de départ (<code>from pipeline.… import …</code>) : ces imports marchent ici.</li><li><code>spark</code> est une SparkSession déjà prête : ne crée pas la tienne.</li><li><code>input_dir</code> est le <b>chemin d'un dossier</b> (une chaîne). Ses fichiers sont listés à gauche de l'éditeur sous <b>Files</b> : clique sur un fichier pour le lire (lecture seule), puis sur <code>report.py</code> pour revenir à ton code. Lis les fichiers à partir de <code>input_dir</code>, jamais d'un chemin écrit à la main : chaque test donne un autre dossier.</li></ol><h5>Ce que tu dois renvoyer</h5><ol><li>Ta fonction doit <b>renvoyer</b> le résultat avec <code>return</code>. Un <code>print</code> ou un <code>show()</code> ne compte pas : il sort seulement dans l'onglet <b>Console Output</b>.</li><li>Le DataFrame renvoyé doit avoir <b>exactement 5 colonnes</b>, avec ces noms et dans cet ordre : <code>hour</code>, <code>endpoint</code>, <code>requests</code>, <code>errors</code>, <code>bytes</code>. Une colonne en plus (une colonne de calcul oubliée), un nom différent ou un autre ordre et le test est faux, même si les valeurs sont justes. Finis par un <code>select(...)</code> de ces colonnes.</li><li><b>L'ordre des lignes compte</b> : trie exactement comme l'énoncé le demande (<code>orderBy</code> à la fin).</li></ol><h5>Lancer et vérifier</h5><ol><li>Spark tourne dans ton Codespace : au premier RUN TESTS de la séance, attends qu'il soit prêt (1 à 2 minutes). Ensuite chaque lancement prend quelques secondes.</li><li>Clique <b>RUN TESTS</b> (ou <code>Ctrl + Entrée</code>) : tes 2 tests visibles tournent.</li><li>Quand c'est bon, tu vois en vert <b>« Tests passed: 2/2. »</b>. Sinon, ouvre le test marqué <b>Wrong answer</b> : compare « Expected return value » et « Your return value », et clique <b>VIEW DIFF</b> pour colorer les cases différentes. <b>Runtime error</b> veut dire que ton code a planté : le message est dans le test.</li><li>Clique <b>SUBMIT</b> : ta note sur 100 compte aussi 6 tests cachés. Soumets dès que quelques tests passent, puis améliore et resoumets : c'est la dernière soumission qui compte.</li></ol>","files":{"root":"input_dir","files":{"eu/access.log":"10.0.0.1 - - [04/Mar/2026:10:12:01 +0100] \"GET /api/orders/42/?full=1 HTTP/1.1\" 200 512 \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"\n10.0.0.2 - - [04/Mar/2026:09:59:00 +0000] \"GET /api/orders/7 HTTP/1.1\" 503 - \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"\n10.0.0.3 - ana [04/Mar/2026:10:05:00 +0100] \"GET / HTTP/1.1\" 200 100 \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"\n10.0.0.4 - - [04/Mar/2026:10:06:00 +0100] \"GET /api/orders/42 HTTP/1.1\" 200 300 \"-\" \"Googlebot/2.1\"\nserver started on :8080\n10.0.0.9 - - [04/Mar/2026:10:0\n","us/2026/03/access.log":"10.1.0.1 - - [04/Mar/2026:04:30:00 -0500] \"POST /api/users/12/orders/3 HTTP/1.1\" 500 80 \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"\n10.1.0.2 - - [04/Mar/2026:09:10:00 -0500] \"GET /api/users/v2 HTTP/1.1\" 200 40 \"-\" \"curl/8.0\"\n","us/notes.txt":"rotate weekly\n"}},"visible":[{"input":{"input_dir":{"__dir__":{"eu/access.log":"10.0.0.1 - - [04/Mar/2026:10:12:01 +0100] \"GET /api/orders/42/?full=1 HTTP/1.1\" 200 512 \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"\n10.0.0.2 - - [04/Mar/2026:09:59:00 +0000] \"GET /api/orders/7 HTTP/1.1\" 503 - \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"\n10.0.0.3 - ana [04/Mar/2026:10:05:00 +0100] \"GET / HTTP/1.1\" 200 100 \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"\n10.0.0.4 - - [04/Mar/2026:10:06:00 +0100] \"GET /api/orders/42 HTTP/1.1\" 200 300 \"-\" \"Googlebot/2.1\"\nserver started on :8080\n10.0.0.9 - - [04/Mar/2026:10:0\n","us/2026/03/access.log":"10.1.0.1 - - [04/Mar/2026:04:30:00 -0500] \"POST /api/users/12/orders/3 HTTP/1.1\" 500 80 \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"\n10.1.0.2 - - [04/Mar/2026:09:10:00 -0500] \"GET /api/users/v2 HTTP/1.1\" 200 40 \"-\" \"curl/8.0\"\n","us/notes.txt":"rotate weekly\n"}}},"expected":{"columns":["hour","endpoint","requests","errors","bytes"],"rows":[["2026-03-04 09:00","/",1,0,100],["2026-03-04 09:00","/api/orders/{id}",2,1,512],["2026-03-04 09:00","/api/users/{id}/orders/{id}",1,1,80],["2026-03-04 14:00","/api/users/v2",1,0,40]]}},{"input":{"input_dir":{"__dir__":{"x.log":"1.1.1.1 - - [01/Jan/2026:00:30:00 +0200] \"GET /health/ HTTP/1.1\" 200 2 \"-\" \"Mozilla/5.0\"\n"}}},"expected":{"columns":["hour","endpoint","requests","errors","bytes"],"rows":[["2025-12-31 22:00","/health",1,0,2]]}}],"hidden_n":6,"hidden":"","solution":""}